WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.640
Can I show you how in just 3 hours I

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:06.800
used AI to write a novel that is

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.920
actually good and how I also created a

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.360
cover, set it to be ready to publish on

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:13.800
Amazon Kindle, and also show you how I

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.760
optimized it for free traffic? Well,

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:17.480
that's today's challenge. So, thank you

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.440
Scrivener for sponsoring today's video.

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.880
More on them later, but for now, let's

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.960
get started. And I'll tell you

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.480
something. I was inspired to try this

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:28.040
after I read this article about an indie

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:31.640
author who in just 1 year published more

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:35.080
than 200 novels using Claude AI and sold

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:36.880
over 50,000 copies. Crazy, right? And

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:38.800
she did so by selling them through

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:41.760
Amazon's self-publishing service called

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:44.120
KDP. You can upload a book PDF file, and

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:47.000
then you can sell it on Amazon as either

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:49.120
an ebook, a print book, or both. And for

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.960
her, she uploaded and sold romance

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:54.040
novels. However, in this previous video

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:56.040
on this channel, I already wrote an AI

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:58.440
romance novel. So, to keep things

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:00.440
interesting for this challenge, I'll

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:02.960
write a different type of book, a

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:04.960
detective novel. And I'll be using a new

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:07.120
and improved AI workflow that I have

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.920
developed to write and publish Kindle

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:12.200
ebooks. So, first up, I researched a

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.160
high-demand, low-competition niche to

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:16.400
write my book in. Because you see, as I

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.640
will discuss later, I like to focus on

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.560
free traffic methods for my marketing.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:22.640
And on Amazon, the search algorithm can

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.840
be a great source of free traffic. My

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.800
goal is to pick a book topic where if

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.440
somebody searches for it, my book will

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:31.400
likely show up near the top of the

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:33.280
results. But this won't happen if I pick

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:35.400
a niche like this one that has lots of

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:37.760
books in it already. So, to find a

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:40.360
low-competition niche, I use the first

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:42.760
tool in my AI workflow, Claude. As my

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:45.640
regular viewers know, Claude is an AI

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:47.760
LLM chatbot similar to ChatGPT. It is

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:49.960
made by a company called Anthropic. And

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:52.360
in writing tests, Claude consistently

00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:54.720
beats ChatGPT. So, I'll be using it to

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:56.480
write my book. Plus, it also has a great

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:58.920
research tool. So, I told it that I

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:01.000
wanted it to write a mystery novel and

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.880
that I wanted it to search the internet

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:04.560
looking for discussions where people

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.320
have been talking about mystery novel

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:09.000
niches that they want more of. And then

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:11.360
in the chat settings, I turned on

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:13.320
research mode and I set it to the latest

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:15.520
Sonnet model, which is the mid-tier

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:17.320
model with thinking turned on. And then

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:19.200
that was it. Claude followed up with a

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:21.480
few clarifying questions, which I

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.440
answered, and then I just sat back and

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:25.080
let Claude search the internet for me.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.080
And it searched through hundreds of

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:29.520
websites. And when it was done, it

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:32.080
created a research document and it found

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:34.920
lots of low competition niches. And out

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:36.720
of these ideas, I decided that this one

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:38.520
would be fun. I figured I'd write a

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.240
murder mystery with two middle-aged

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:44.400
sisters who accidentally become crime

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:46.320
solvers. And I had another idea. So, a

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:48.440
popular hobby in this demographic is

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:51.000
travel, right? So, what about a murder

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:53.680
mystery but on a plane? So, I did a

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.280
search for it on Amazon and look, you

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:58.280
get almost no results. So, if we were to

00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:00.280
write a book in this niche, our book

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:02.120
would most likely immediately jump to

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:04.040
the top of the search results. Plus,

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:06.640
there is another reason I like this

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:08.480
idea. It's a fun, almost cheesy concept,

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:10.400
which means it falls into the beach

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:12.880
fiction genre, which are basically

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:15.080
feel-good books that are simple and easy

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:17.239
to read. In other words, they're cozy

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:19.760
fiction. And while AI doesn't usually

00:03:17.239 --> 00:03:22.080
write award-winning books, it can be

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:24.040
great at writing cozy fiction. Plus,

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.519
I've also noticed that on Amazon, indie

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:28.880
publishers can do very well financially

00:03:26.519 --> 00:03:30.720
by creating a book series. So, take this

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:32.680
murder mystery series, it's set on a

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.320
cruise ship. In each book, the cruise

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:36.560
ship director dissolves different

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:38.720
murders. People love it. So, these

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:41.120
traveling sisters I've created could

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:43.360
become their own series, too. All right,

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:45.680
then. We have our book idea. So, now to

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:47.560
move on to the next step. But first,

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:50.040
just a quick tip. In this video, I'll be

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:52.960
using the pro version of Claude and the

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:55.080
deep research tool I used is not in the

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:56.640
free version. So, if you want a deep

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:58.920
research tool that you can access for

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:01.240
free, check out Perplexity. This is the

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:02.920
most popular AI-powered search engine.

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:05.160
The free version of this has a deep

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:07.440
research tool built into it. You get

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:09.560
some free searches each month. Out of

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:11.240
interest, I ran the same search with it,

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:14.360
and it gave me similar results to

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:16.720
Claude. But yes, either way, now that

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:19.239
I've picked my niche, I then moved on to

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:21.560
a crucial phase that most people skip,

00:04:19.239 --> 00:04:24.240
which is this: plotting. Because you

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:25.960
see, AI novels are usually terrible,

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:28.600
since most people just come to their

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:31.919
favorite AI, like ChatGPT, and say,

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:34.560
"Hey, write me chapter one of my book.

00:04:31.919 --> 00:04:36.400
Go." And that's it. No planning,

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:38.320
nothing. And that is a legitimate

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:40.800
approach some human authors take, which

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:42.400
is called the pantser approach. But AIs

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.880
work much better at writing when you

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:47.360
instead take the plotter approach, which

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:49.280
is to plan your book before you write

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:51.480
it. And to do that, we'll be using a

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:53.000
feature within Claude called projects.

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:55.480
This lets you create a dedicated

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.680
workspace where users can create chats

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:59.960
and upload files to it that Claude will

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:02.280
essentially remember and be able to

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:04.880
access from conversation to conversation

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:06.880
within it. It's super handy. So, I set

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:09.440
up a project for my book. Then, within

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:12.040
this project, I started a new chat where

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:13.960
I started working backwards. Plotting is

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:15.720
usually easiest if you figure out what

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:17.640
your ending will be first and work

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:19.600
backwards from there. So, I got Claude

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:21.520
to come up with some twist ending ideas

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:24.120
for me. And I'll be honest, I didn't

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:26.520
like them much. So, I got it to come up

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:27.720
with some more, and out of these, I

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:30.040
liked the idea of it being some

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:32.200
combination of it being the pilot that

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:34.400
did it and that he had set the murder

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:36.360
plot up in advance, knowing that the

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.919
passenger would be on the plane. So, I

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:39.960
asked Claude to expand more on this

00:05:37.919 --> 00:05:42.080
idea, and it came up with some ideas

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:44.120
behind the pilot's potential motivations

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:46.360
and scenarios for how he did it, too.

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:48.360
So, I read over these, and I picked the

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:50.200
ideas that I liked the most, like the

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:52.200
fact that the pilot caused an allergic

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:54.800
reaction, and I asked Claude to take

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:56.880
this and to expand out on it into a full

00:05:54.800 --> 00:05:58.840
reference document. And not only did it

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.920
write this reference document for me,

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:03.200
but it coded it together, too. And it

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:04.720
had pretty much everything you'd expect,

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:07.480
an explanation of the history of the

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:09.600
motive, an explanation of how he did it,

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:12.200
and even read hearings that distract

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:14.120
from him. So, I downloaded this, and

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:16.920
then I uploaded it into the project to

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:19.160
save it as a reference. Then next up was

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:21.000
the characters. So, I started a new chat

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:22.440
in the project and asked it to come up

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:24.240
with some character profiles for the

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:26.000
characters that we would expect in our

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:27.800
book. And it came up with some fun

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:30.160
characters, perfect for the style of

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:31.880
book I was after. So, I then asked it to

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:34.080
turn these character profiles into a

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:36.320
document, which it coded together for

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:38.560
me, no problem. And if you want to make

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:40.200
any changes to these, it's really easy.

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:42.760
Like, reading back through it, Claude

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:44.640
had made Eleanor the organized sister a

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:46.840
retired teacher, but I thought it would

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:48.400
be better if she'd been a lawyer. So, I

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:50.760
asked Claude to make this change and

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:52.520
updated the document for me. So then, I

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:54.440
downloaded it, and then I uploaded it to

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:56.360
the project files, and then we'd one

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:58.200
more thing to do, and that was to choose

00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:59.720
a storyline structure. These are plot

00:06:58.200 --> 00:07:01.760
structures that have been proven to be

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:03.560
popular with readers. The hero's journey

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:05.360
is a classic one, although the save the

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:07.720
cat structure is probably the most

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:09.880
popular for commercial fiction. It works

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:12.400
well within a lot of genres, including

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:15.120
romance, but since I was writing a

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:17.560
detective novel, I chose this structure.

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:19.600
The 12-step mystery formula. Then I just

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:21.680
came to Claude and I started a new chat

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:24.120
within our project asking it to write

00:07:21.680 --> 00:07:26.440
out a 12-step mystery formula based on

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:28.720
the documents we had created so far. And

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:30.880
that is what it did. It both wrote it

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:33.080
and coded it together into a document

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:35.360
for me. And I read over it, and it was

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:37.320
pretty good. So, I just downloaded this

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:40.240
formula document, and I uploaded it to

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:42.000
the project. Awesome. So, now that that

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:43.760
finished plotting, I then moved on to

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:46.200
the next step, which was to actually

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:47.640
write my book. And actually, because of

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:50.160
all the plotting that I had already

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:52.680
done, this was surprisingly simple. Yep,

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:54.880
basically, I just started a new chat and

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:56.960
asked it to write chapter one based off

00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:59.040
of our project plotting documents. And I

00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:01.240
copied and pasted in part one of my

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:03.360
12-step mystery formula for it to use as

00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:05.800
a reference. And then that was it. I sat

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:07.360
back and let AI work its magic, and 3

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:09.520
minutes later, Claude had finished

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:11.880
coding together a document containing

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:14.320
chapter one. Now, this is very

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:16.160
important. I didn't just get AI to write

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:18.520
the book for me. I treated this as a

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:20.280
collaborative process. Basically, I read

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:22.000
over it to check for any changes I

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:23.760
wanted or mistakes that it might have

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:25.919
made. And I noticed that the chapter

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:27.480
ended with them in the plane, whereas as

00:08:25.919 --> 00:08:29.640
part of the plot we'd written in our

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:32.360
12-step mystery formula, they shouldn't

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:34.240
begin to board until chapter two. So, I

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:36.919
asked it to change this, which it did

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:38.440
for me, no problem. Then, after that, I

00:08:36.919 --> 00:08:40.320
just repeated this process for each of

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:42.479
my chapters. And I kept it all within

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:44.640
the same chat thread. I ended up writing

00:08:42.479 --> 00:08:46.280
15 chapters this way. And once it had

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:48.720
finished them, I downloaded each

00:08:46.280 --> 00:08:50.680
chapter, and I started a new chat, and I

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:52.200
uploaded the chapters into it and asked

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:54.520
Claude to turn them into a single

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:56.240
manuscript while fixing up any mistakes

00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:58.600
it noticed along the way. And when I

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:01.240
did, I made sure to change the Claude

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:03.600
model from Sonnet to Opus. This is

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:06.000
Claude's most advanced model. It's very

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:09.200
smart and can handle a lots of text and

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:11.600
data. But, since it's smart, it uses

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:14.800
lots of tokens, so eats into your usage

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:17.040
limits faster. So, to save usage, I got

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:19.360
the medium model Sonnet to write my

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:21.120
first draft chapter by chapter, and then

00:09:19.360 --> 00:09:23.480
once I started making complex

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:26.080
manuscript-wide edits, I switched to

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:28.240
Opus. It took all of my chapters and

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:31.480
turned them into a full draft book for

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:33.480
me. Nice. So, now I was ready for the

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:35.240
next step. But first, we have a quick

00:09:33.480 --> 00:09:37.640
word from today's video sponsor,

